course: dictionary
lesson: 7318
title: The beach that breaks the rule
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Plage is a French word, and it means beach. «Je vais à la plage» means «I am going to the beach». Plage is not an everyday English word, but you may spot it in French lessons, on French road signs and in names of seaside places.
explain: Every French noun is either masculine or feminine. Plage is feminine, so it goes with la or une: «la plage» (the beach), «une plage» (a beach), and in the plural «des plages» (beaches).
explain: Endings give clues, but they are not rules. «Most words ending in -age are masculine (le fromage, le garage, le voyage), but la plage, la page, la cage and l'image are feminine exceptions.» Plage ends in -age and is still feminine, so a good detective learns it as la plage.
fact: Plage is the French word for beach.
fact: Plage is feminine: la plage, une plage, des plages (plural).
fact: Most words ending in -age are masculine, but plage is a famous exception.
fact: In astronomy, a plage is a bright patch in the Sun's atmosphere near a sunspot. This is a rare, science meaning.
fact: You may see plage in names such as Paris-Plages, a seaside place, so it keeps its beach meaning there too.
exercise: «Je vais à la plage.» What does plage mean? | A forest path | A market stall | *A beach
exercise: Is plage masculine or feminine? | Masculine, like le voyage | Masculine, because it ends in -age | *Feminine, even though it ends in -age | Neither, because it is an English word
exercise: Which is the plural of plage? | le plages | la plage | *des plages | une plages
test: Which article goes with plage in the sentence «___ plage est belle»? | le | les | *la | du
test: In science, what is a plage? | A dark spot on the Moon | A large wave in the sea | *A bright patch in the Sun's atmosphere near a sunspot | A kind of cloud over mountains
exam: Which statement is correct? | Plage has no article in French | Plage is a German word for sea | Plage ends in -age, so it must be masculine, like le voyage | *Plage ends in -age but is feminine, like la page and la cage
